1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
A story imagines a world that does not exist yet.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
A product has to work in the world that does.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Picture the product in two dimensions. Behavior is what it does.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Structure is how it is organized to make that behavior possible.

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Time adds a third dimension: the product moves from one state to another.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:39,000
The backlog holds possible changes along that journey.

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Each story begins with something that matters to someone: a frustration, a hope, a desire.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
It invites us to imagine a change worth making.

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,000
That is its intended impact: a difference in someone’s world.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,000
The person wanting that change does not think in our features or components.

11
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
One story may change several features and reach across several components.

12
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
And one feature may change through many stories over time.

13
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,000
A feature is part of the product’s state. A story proposes a transition.

14
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,000
When we pursue that story, it has another kind of impact: on the product itself.

15
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
The change can disturb existing behavior and strain the structure that supports it.

16
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Development must assimilate that impact into the whole product.

17
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
We reconcile the new behavior with what the product already does.

18
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
We reshape the structure so the changed product fits together.

19
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
This takes judgment. What matters? What should change? What must still hold?

20
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
We resolve those questions into decisions about behavior and design.

21
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Those decisions become explicit in the code, tests, and product description.

22
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
The result is a coherent product with new capabilities and a structure that supports them.

23
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
It has changed. The story’s impact is now part of how the product works.

24
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
The story has done its job. It becomes a spent story.

25
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Its history remains available when we need to revisit a decision.

26
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Understanding today’s product should not require retracing every story that shaped it.

27
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Stories help us imagine change. Products embody the decisions that make it real.

28
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:57,000
The story is spent. The change remains. The product is ready to evolve again.
